% ============================================================================
% volcano-type — three named types of volcano and what each actually is
% (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% Not every volcano looks the same: a cinder cone is the simplest kind, a
% shield volcano is built almost entirely of fluid lava flows, and a
% composite volcano (a stratovolcano) is one of the grandest mountain-
% building kinds. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? volcano_type(shield_volcano, $D)   % built_almost_entirely_of_fluid_lava_flows
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `volcano_type(type, description)` carrying the source citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a volcano-related term that is not one of these three. The
% query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and recall the type it
% names:
%
%     ? volcano_type($T, is_the_simplest_type_of_volcano)   % cinder_cone
%
% The three volcano types, as a little truth table:
%
%     type                 description                                       quote (verbatim)
%     -------------------  ------------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     cinder_cone           is_the_simplest_type_of_volcano                   "Cinder cones are the simplest type of volcano."
%     shield_volcano        built_almost_entirely_of_fluid_lava_flows         "Shield volcanoes are built almost entirely of fluid lava flows."
%     composite_volcano     also_called_a_stratovolcano                       "Some of the Earth's grandest mountains are composite volcanoes—sometimes called stratovolcanoes."
%
% Provenance: quoted from USGS's "About Volcanoes" page, which introduces
% each of these three types in its own complete sentence before going on
% to further description. The original authoring note said the quotes were
% "WebFetch-verified before writing (twice ...)"; the raw-page measurement
% below supersedes it, and two of the quotes above are not the page's
% characters (see below). `trust authoritative` — USGS is
% a primary .gov source, the same tier the sibling `rock-type.adj`/
% `mineral-hardness.adj` (the other libraries in this directory) already
% use for their USGS citations. A volcano-related term that is NOT one of
% these three — "lava_dome", a real term the same page also names, but one
% the source ITSELF explicitly disclaims as not a type: "A volcano can
% host many lava domes over a long period of time, so these are
% technically not a "volcano type" but rather an eruption phenomenon." —
% is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather than
% inventing a description. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SENTENCE (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, #14986). The envelope
% used to be the shield row's own sentence, the primary source of all three
% answers. Each quoted sentence above names its type and its description, so
% every row now takes its own as a `source`. The envelope is now a framing
% sentence from the page, which names no type:
%
%     "There are about 1,350 potentially active volcanoes worldwide, not
%      counting the volcanoes under the oceans."
%
% Measured 2026-09-15 on the raw page (HTTP 200; a nonsense path on the same
% host returns 404), with inline tags removed without a space and only ASCII
% whitespace collapsed: the three row sentences and the envelope each occur
% exactly once, each inside a `<p>`. Every row's page is the envelope's, so
% no row restates a `locator` line or `trust` (ADJ-TABLES §4).
%
% TWO QUOTES ABOVE ARE NOT THE PAGE'S CHARACTERS, and the shipped envelope
% occurred on no page because of it. The page writes U+00A0 (NO-BREAK SPACE)
% after "Cinder" in the cinder-cone sentence, and on both sides of "lava" in
% the shield sentence ("fluid lava flows."). A comment cannot show U+00A0,
% so the truth table keeps plain spaces; in that form each sentence occurs
% zero times. The rows carry the page's characters. The lava-dome quote used
% single quotes around volcano type; the page writes double quotes, and the
% quote above now does too.
% ============================================================================

table volcano_type {
    columns type, description

    row (cinder_cone, is_the_simplest_type_of_volcano) {
        source "Cinder cones are the simplest type of volcano."
    }
    row (shield_volcano, built_almost_entirely_of_fluid_lava_flows) {
        source "Shield volcanoes are built almost entirely of fluid lava flows."
    }
    row (composite_volcano, also_called_a_stratovolcano) {
        source "Some of the Earth's grandest mountains are composite volcanoes—sometimes called stratovolcanoes."
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE WAS THE SHIELD ROW'S OWN SENTENCE, the primary source of
    % all three answers, and as shipped it occurred on no page (plain spaces
    % where the page writes U+00A0). It is now a framing sentence that names
    % no type. Every row overrides it, so it reaches no answer.
    source "There are about 1,350 potentially active volcanoes worldwide, not counting the volcanoes under the oceans."
    locator "https://www.usgs.gov/programs/VHP/about-volcanoes"
    trust authoritative
}
